## 2. The WS endpoint is a connector, not a plugin — and it LISTENS where its siblings DIAL

### [CXC-WS-CONNECTOR-NOT-PLUGIN] MUST be registered as a `transport_registry.py` connector, `type: ws`; MUST NOT be a `ComposePlugin`

- requirement: (MUST, testable) the WS endpoint MUST be implemented as a new factory registered under
  `type: ws` in the SAME `_FACTORIES` registry `transport_registry.py` already uses for
  `mqtt`/`can`/`uart`/`synthetic`/`replay` (`register("ws", _ws)`), and MUST NOT be a `ComposePlugin`
  subclass under [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]. A declared `type: ws` that the registry cannot build MUST
  raise `UnknownTransportError` exactly as an unknown `type:` does today — no silent local-only
  degradation.
- inherits: the connector contract — SCOPE (does this cross the membrane, [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE])
  and ENCODE (the wire format, [CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT]) only, never a third role. [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-NEVER-TRANSFORMS]'s
  membrane-not-mediator rule applies to this connector exactly as it does to every other one; the WS
  endpoint does not derive a new fact from what it forwards.
- the-inversion-a-builder-must-not-miss: (architect-synthesis, not David's word — an implementation
  consequence of the ruling, named explicitly so it is not silently discovered mid-build) every other
  registered factory DIALS a fabric that already exists independently of the kernel (an MQTT broker,
  a CAN interface, a serial port). The WS connector has no such fabric to dial — there is no broker
  between a browser tab and the kernel — so its factory instead OPENS A LISTEN SOCKET and each
  accepted connection becomes one peer ([CXC-WS-SINGLETON-PEER]). The declared-config idiom
  (`type: ws`, a `spec` dict, a built object handed back to the caller) is unchanged; only the socket's
  DIRECTION inverts. A builder assuming "connect like MQTT" would build a client dialing nothing and
  ship a connector that never accepts a browser.
- socket-scope: the listen socket is opened ONCE at the ComposeServer/kernel level ([CXC-CS-MICROKERNEL]),
  never once per tenant — [CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY] specifies why.
- negative-control: an unrecognized `type: ws2` (or any other unregistered name) still raises
  `UnknownTransportError` naming `known_types()`, and `known_types()` after this connector registers
  MUST include `"ws"` alongside the five existing entries.
- verify-machine: `transport_registry.known_types()` includes `"ws"`; `transport_registry.build({"type": "ws", ...})`
  returns a WS connector object, never `None` and never silently falls through to local-only, on a
  spec with a valid listen `host`/`port`; `transport_registry.build({"type": "ws"})` with no host/port
  either applies a documented default or raises naming the missing field — never silently binds to an
  unintended interface.
- verify-human: none required — this node's failure surface is fully machine-observable.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — David's actual word (line 25906): "I agree it's a connector." That is the
  whole of David's connector ruling. The elaboration that follows in this node's title and requirement
  — "CONNECTOR in the transport family, NOT a plugin — a new `transport_registry.py` factory. It
  inherits the connector contract: scope + encode, never transform." — is the author's framing of the
  (`docs/composeserver/AUDIT-ws-endpoint.md`, finding C3) and [DEC-WS-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-24]. The
  `the-inversion-a-builder-must-not-miss` property is architect-synthesis, flagged as such and not
  attributed to David.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-CONTRACT], [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-NEVER-TRANSFORMS], [CXC-CS-MICROKERNEL]

---

## 3. A WS client is a singleton persistent peer — not an ephemeral factory

### [CXC-WS-SINGLETON-PEER] MUST treat each accepted connection as one singleton persistent peer following the peripheral-attach lifecycle

- requirement: (MUST, testable) each accepted WS connection MUST be represented by exactly ONE
  long-lived peer object for the lifetime of that socket — never re-created per message, never pooled,
  never treated as a stateless request/response handler. The peer object owns the connection's
  lifecycle state ([CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]), its declared subscription set ([CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION]), and its
  identity ([CXC-WS-IDENTITY-POLICY]) for as long as the socket is open.
- claim-many-tabs-is-not-a-new-shape: (architect-synthesis, elaborating David's own line-25950 question
  into a ruling — David's actual words were a question, not this elaboration; see `provenance` below)
  many browser tabs connecting to the same ComposeServer are many INDEPENDENT singleton peers over ONE
  listening transport — structurally the same shape as MQTT serving N devices, not a new concept the WS
  connector must invent. An ESP32 also joins and leaves a bus; a tab joining and leaving is the
  identical multiplicity pattern realized in a browser instead of firmware. The peripheral-attach
  lifecycle ([CXC-CBK-PERIPHERAL-PLUGIN-MODEL]'s pub+sub-for-control case — a WS peer publishes AND
  subscribes, so it is the control shape, not the pure-realization subscribe-only shape) governs a WS
  peer exactly as it governs any other peripheral.
- negative-control: TWO simultaneous connections from the SAME browser (two tabs) MUST produce TWO
  independent peer objects with independent lifecycle state, independent subscriptions, and (per
  [CXC-WS-IDENTITY-POLICY]) independent or shared identity per that node's policy — never one shared
  mutable peer object silently serving both sockets.
- verify-machine: opening two WS connections against a test server asserts two distinct peer object
  identities, each with its own subscription state; closing one MUST NOT affect the other's lifecycle
  state or subscriptions.
- verify-human: a maker opens two browser tabs against a real ComposeServer, subscribes each to a
  different namespace family, and confirms (via `_server/status` or equivalent) two independent peer
  entries — closing one tab leaves the other's subscriptions and bound tenant untouched.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — David raised the singleton-peer idea himself, as a question (line 25950):
  "Actually, isn't each tab in a browser a singleton persistent peer, essentially a separate ESP32 w/SPI
  display, pointing to a different ComposeBus?" The node's title, the "NOT an 'ephemeral factory'"
  framing, and the `claim-many-tabs-is-not-a-new-shape` elaboration above (the MQTT-with-N-devices
  multiplicity argument) are the author's framing answering David's question in the affirmative, not
  (`docs/composeserver/AUDIT-ws-endpoint.md`, finding C3) and [DEC-WS-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-24]. David's
  Q1 answer (2026-08-24, same decision) confirms each such peer binds to a bus THIS server itself
  instantiates and manages — never "a different ComposeServer entirely," which was the architect's
  over-reach in raising the question's own follow-on framing, not David's word.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-PERIPHERAL-PLUGIN-MODEL], [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]

---

## 4. Connection lifecycle — connect, pre-binding, select, bound, switch

### [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE] MUST implement the fixed four-state lifecycle on one socket: PRE-BINDING, BOUND, and the SWITCH transition between two BOUND states

- requirement: (MUST, testable) a peer's lifecycle state MUST be exactly one of: `PRE-BINDING`
  (connected, no focal tenant bus selected — the peer cannot publish or subscribe to any tenant
  namespace yet); `BOUND` (a focal tenant bus is selected; the peer is a peer of THAT bus per
  [CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY]). A `SWITCH` is a transition FROM `BOUND` (tenant A) THROUGH an explicit
  leave-A step TO `BOUND` (tenant B) — never a direct A-to-B splice that skips the leave. There is no
  fifth state; a peer that disconnects is simply gone, not parked in a state.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) while `PRE-BINDING`, the peer MUST NOT receive ANY tenant-namespace
  traffic and MUST NOT be able to publish to any tenant namespace — the only traffic legal in this
  state is the bus-selection exchange itself (the operator list, [CXC-WS-ENTRY-OPERATOR], or a
  deep-link's pre-selection, [CXC-WS-ENTRY-DEEPLINK]). This is [CXC-SUB-DECLARE]'s empty-declared-set
  default-deny applied at the CONNECTION level, before any subscription has even been declared.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) entering `BOUND` for tenant T MUST perform, IN ORDER: (1) resolve T
  to a live `ComposeIOBus` via the SAME per-tenant lookup [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY] already provides
  (`ComposeServer.tenant_bus(T)` or equivalent, reused unchanged — this spec does not reimplement
  tenant resolution); (2) apply the peer's declared subscription set against T
  ([CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION]); (3) perform retained-state replay for every namespace the declared set
  matches ([CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY]). Replay MUST happen only AFTER the subscription is applied, never
  before — replaying against an unset subscription would either leak (replay everything) or drop
  (replay nothing), and both are the exact silent-wrong-answer class [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS] warns
  against.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) a `SWITCH` from tenant A to tenant B MUST: (1) unsubscribe the peer's
  entire declared set from A's bus (a clean leave — no residual callback fires after this step);
  (2) transition the peer's lifecycle state to `PRE-BINDING` momentarily (never directly A-BOUND to
  B-BOUND with no intermediate state a test can observe); (3) re-run the `BOUND`-entry sequence above
  against B. The peer's declared subscription SET (the namespace patterns, not the bus) is carried
  across the switch unchanged, UNLESS the operator UX ([DD-WS-SWITCH-UX], out of scope here) offers a
  re-declare step — this carry-forward default is (architect-synthesis, NOT part of the david-ruling;
  David left the switch mechanism unspecified, see `q2-settled-scope` below) a choice this spec makes
  for the open [DD-WS-SWITCH-UX] fork, revisitable when that fork resolves.
- q2-settled-scope: (david-ruling, 2026-08-24 Q2 answer, [DEC-WS-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-24]) David's "a
  mechanism we have yet to specify" (line 25961) was NARROW — it named only the operator-facing web-page
  UX TRIGGER for a switch ([DD-WS-SWITCH-UX]), not this node's connector-side leave-A/pre-binding/join-B
  ORDERING, which therefore stays SETTLED and david-endorsed. It does not overrule or reopen the
  ordering requirements above; only [DD-WS-SWITCH-UX] (the UI trigger, and whether it re-declares
  subscriptions) remains deferred.
- conceptual-precedent-named-not-reused: the SWITCH is the same CATEGORY of move as device repoint
  ([SPEC:CXC-REPOINT], config-over-bus) — a live re-pointing operation, not a firmware change — but it
  is NOT a literal reuse of that spec's retained-binding/commit-confirmed/trial-timer machinery, which
  solves a DIFFERENT problem (which MQTT BROKER a device dials). This spec's switch is tenant
  RESELECTION within one already-open kernel socket; conflating the two would import commit-confirmed
  ceremony this lightweight, in-session UX move does not need. [DD-WS-SWITCH-UX] is the fork for the
  switch's own concrete UX mechanism.
- scenario:
  - when: a peer connects, receives the operator bus list, selects tenant `haven`, then later selects
    tenant `bsg` via the switch mechanism
  - then: the peer is `PRE-BINDING` immediately after connect; `BOUND(haven)` after selection, with
    retained replay for its declared set against `haven`'s current namespace values; briefly
    `PRE-BINDING` again during the switch (observable in a test via an intermediate-state assertion,
    mirroring [CXC-SUB-SUPERSET]'s "reconfiguration passes through a wider/named intermediate state,
    never an ambiguous one" discipline); `BOUND(bsg)` after the switch completes, with retained replay
    re-run against `bsg`
- verify-machine: a test peer asserts `PRE-BINDING` immediately post-connect; asserts NO tenant
  namespace callback fires before a bus is selected; asserts `BOUND(T)` triggers exactly one replay
  pass over the declared set against T's current values; asserts a switch A->B unsubscribes every A
  callback (a spy asserts zero further A-tenant callbacks post-switch) before any B callback fires.
- verify-human: David or composeio-first-bringup opens a real browser tab against a real
  multi-tenant ComposeServer, selects a tenant from the operator list, observes namespace values
  populate immediately (not after the next publish — the replay gate), switches tenants, and confirms
  the view goes to the NEW tenant's current state with no residual OLD-tenant value lingering on
  screen.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — David's actual words (line 25961), in his own plain language, not the
  state names below: "if I connect to ComposeServer, it seems like the natural first thing to do (after
  some login ceremony that we have yet to define) would be to present a list of ComposeBusses. Then I'd
  choose which one I want to manage ... that'd be the default for that tab until I close the tab or
  explicitly change the focal ComposeBus (through a mechanism we have yet to specify)." The state names
  PRE-BINDING/BOUND/SWITCH and the "config-over-bus REPOINT" mapping are the author's framing of the
  agreed ruling, not David's own vocabulary; retagged 2026-08-24 per the audit
  (`docs/composeserver/AUDIT-ws-endpoint.md`, finding C3) and [DEC-WS-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-24]. David's
  Q2 answer (same decision) clarifies that "a mechanism we have yet to specify" named only the UI
  trigger ([DD-WS-SWITCH-UX]) — see `q2-settled-scope` above; the connector-side leave/join ordering
  itself is settled. The ORDERING requirement inside `BOUND`-entry (subscribe-before-replay) and the
  intermediate-state requirement inside `SWITCH` are architect-synthesis, derived from
  [CXC-SUB-SUPERSET]'s reconfiguration discipline, not David's stated word — as is the carry-forward
  subscription default flagged inline above (audit finding C4).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY], [CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION], [CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY], [SPEC:CXC-REPOINT], [DD-WS-SWITCH-UX]

---

## 5. Multi-tenancy — one tenant at a time, the kernel is the sole cross-tenant surface

### [CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY] MUST bind a connection to exactly ONE tenant at a time; MUST NEVER let a peer see another tenant's traffic

- requirement: (MUST, testable) at every instant a peer is `BOUND`, it is bound to EXACTLY ONE
  tenant's `ComposeIOBus`. The WS connector's LISTEN SOCKET spans tenants (one accept loop serves
  every tenant, per `the-inversion` property at [CXC-WS-CONNECTOR-NOT-PLUGIN]), but a BOUND peer's
  subscription and publish surface MUST resolve against ONLY its currently-selected tenant bus — this
  is [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY]'s LOCKED-PERMANENTLY "plugins are ALWAYS per-tenant; NO shared instances"
  invariant, applied at the connector's own binding step, exactly as [CXC-CSL-PER-TENANT] applies it
  at the plugin loader's.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) the CROSS-TENANT bus list (which tenants exist, for the operator entry
  mode, [CXC-WS-ENTRY-OPERATOR]) MUST be served by the KERNEL/ComposeServer, never assembled or
  cached by a bound peer's own connection state. A peer's PRE-BINDING view of "what tenants exist" and
  its BOUND view of "what this tenant's namespaces say" are two structurally different surfaces, and
  [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY]'s "cross-tenant views live in the kernel or an external tool, NEVER a plugin"
  rule extends here: never a per-tenant plugin, and — this connector's own addition — never a
  per-connection cache either, because a stale cached list is the exact hand-maintained-list failure
  [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND] catalogs.
- negative-control: a peer `BOUND` to tenant A that declares a subscription pattern matching a
  namespace that ONLY exists on tenant B's bus MUST receive nothing for that pattern — no cross-tenant
  leak, no error, simply no match, because tenant B's namespace registry is never consulted while
  bound to A.
- verify-machine: two tenants each publish to a namespace of the SAME NAME (e.g. `_server/status`) with
  different values; a peer bound to tenant A and subscribed to that namespace receives ONLY A's value,
  never B's, for the duration it is bound to A; after a switch to B it receives ONLY B's value.
- verify-human: an operator inspects two tenants' `_server/status` side by side on real infrastructure
  and confirms a WS peer bound to one never surfaces the other's data, including during a switch
  (no frame of overlap where both are visible).
- q1-scope-confirmed: (david-ruling, 2026-08-24 Q1 answer, [DEC-WS-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-24]) each
  ComposeBus a peer selects from is a tenant bus INSTANTIATED AND MANAGED BY THIS ComposeServer — an
  intra-server, N-tenant model. The architect's earlier "a different ComposeServer entirely" framing
  (raised mid-session, never David's own word) was over-reach and is retracted; this node's
  kernel-served cross-tenant registry is the confirmed home for the selection list, not a
  cross-deployment/federated one.
- invalidating_assumption: "David's own words (line 25921): 'yes, routing to one and only one tenant
  bus, subject to further analysis and re-litigation once we dive into actual multi-tenancy
  deployments.' This node's MUST is FIRM for one-bus-per-connection-AT-AN-INSTANT — a peer is bound to
  exactly one tenant at any given moment, and that binding is switchable ([CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]) — and that
  part does not re-open. What David flagged as re-litigable is the LARGER claim that this
  one-tenant-per-connection MODEL survives real multi-tenancy deployment unchanged; once actual
  multi-tenancy deployments are underway, this node's binding MODEL itself is subject to re-analysis,
  not merely its implementation. See [CXC-WS-ACCEPTANCE]'s WS-V4 for how the gate is scoped to the firm
  part only."
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — "a connection is only ever a peer of ONE tenant at a time; the cross-tenant
  bus list is the KERNEL's sole-cross-tenant surface ([CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY] LOCKED) — the WS
  transport spans tenants at the socket level (kernel-owned), binding is per-tenant, NO plugin/tenant
  sees another." (session ruling #4, explicitly agreed by David.) The re-litigable caveat restored
  2026-08-24 per the audit (`docs/composeserver/AUDIT-ws-endpoint.md`, finding C1) and
  [DEC-WS-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-24] — see `invalidating_assumption` above.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY], [CXC-CSL-PER-TENANT], [CXC-WS-ENTRY-OPERATOR]

---

## 6. Entry modes — operator list and deep-link

### [CXC-WS-ENTRY-OPERATOR] MUST offer an operator entry mode: connect, then present the cross-tenant bus list, then select

- requirement: (MUST, testable) in the absence of a deep-link path ([CXC-WS-ENTRY-DEEPLINK]), a newly
  connected `PRE-BINDING` peer MUST be served the cross-tenant bus list ([CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY]'s
  kernel-served surface) and MUST remain `PRE-BINDING` until it explicitly selects one entry from that
  list. There is no implicit default tenant.
- out-of-scope-named: the bus list's CONTENT (what metadata each tenant entry carries), its selection
  UX, and any reserved auth gate in front of it are the cross-tenant OPERATOR CONSOLE's own unit — this
  spec specifies only that the WS connector MUST serve SOME such list over the same socket in
  `PRE-BINDING` state and MUST honor a selection from it; it does not design the console.
- verify-machine: a peer connecting with no deep-link path receives a non-empty tenant list (against
  a test server with >=2 tenants) before any bus-selection message, and stays `PRE-BINDING` until it
  sends a valid selection.
- verify-human: an operator connects a fresh browser tab with no path/query hint and visually confirms
  a tenant picker appears before any live data, matching the tenants actually configured on the
  server.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — David's actual words (line 25961): "if I connect to ComposeServer, it
  seems like the natural first thing to do (after some login ceremony that we have yet to define) would
  be to present a list of ComposeBusses. Then I'd choose which one I want to manage." This is the ONLY
  entry mode David described. The "two entry modes: (a) operator / (b) deep-link" taxonomy and the
  "(a)"/"(b)" labeling are the author's framing, not David's; retagged 2026-08-24 per the audit
  (`docs/composeserver/AUDIT-ws-endpoint.md`, finding C3) and [DEC-WS-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-24] — see
  [CXC-WS-ENTRY-DEEPLINK]'s provenance for the correction on the second entry mode.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY], [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]

### [CXC-WS-ENTRY-DEEPLINK] MUST offer a deep-link entry mode: a URL-path pre-selects the focal bus, skipping the list

- requirement: (MUST, testable) when a connection carries a recognized deep-link designator (the exact
  URL-path shape is [DD-WS-DEEPLINK-URL], OPEN), the peer MUST transition directly from connect to
  `BOUND` against the designated tenant, WITHOUT the operator list ever being served — both entry
  modes MUST converge on the same `BOUND`-entry sequence ([CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]), so a deep-linked peer
  and an operator-selected peer are indistinguishable once bound.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) a deep-link designator naming a tenant that does not exist MUST fail
  loudly (an explicit error state, connection refused or an in-band error message naming the unknown
  tenant) — never silently fall through to the operator list and never silently bind to a wrong/default
  tenant.
- boundary: the deep-link is the SECONDARY entry, not the primary router — [DD-WS-DEEPLINK-URL]'s
  question is the URL SHAPE only; that a deep-link exists and must converge on the same bound state as
  the operator path is decided here, not open.
- verify-machine: a peer connecting with a valid deep-link designator reaches `BOUND(T)` with no
  operator-list message ever sent to it; one with an unrecognized designator receives an explicit
  named error, never a silent fallback.
- verify-human: a maker follows a real deep-link URL in a browser and lands directly on the named
  tenant's live view with no picker screen shown.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the deep-link entry mode (a URL-path pre-selecting the focal bus,
  skipping the operator list) is the author's addition to the entry-mode taxonomy; David never proposed
  or agreed to a second entry mode. His only nearby agreement (line 25976, "Agree on ii.") was to the
  transport-vs-console SCOPE SPLIT, not to a deep-link mechanism — he described only the operator flow
  (see [CXC-WS-ENTRY-OPERATOR]). That both entry modes must converge on the same BOUND sequence
  ([CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]) is a design requirement this spec imposes for consistency, not something David
  ruled on. Re-tagged from `david-ruling` 2026-08-24 per the audit
  (`docs/composeserver/AUDIT-ws-endpoint.md`, finding C2) and [DEC-WS-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-24].
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [CXC-WS-ENTRY-OPERATOR], [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE], [DD-WS-DEEPLINK-URL]

---

## 7. Auth — reserved, explicitly OUT of v1

### [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE] DEFERRED — auth is OUT of v1; the position is reserved, not designed

- decision: (david-ruling, verbatim) auth is explicitly OUT of v1. David: a login ceremony, multiple
  accounts, per-account namespace-locking are "way way overkill for right now." v1 is local/trusted,
  the cross-tenant bus list is UNFILTERED, no accounts, no permissions.
- what-is-reserved: a future login gate would sit AHEAD of [CXC-WS-ENTRY-OPERATOR]'s bus list — it
  would FILTER which tenants a given peer's list even shows, a server-level gate, not a per-namespace
  permission system layered onto [CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION]. This spec's requirements MUST NOT be built in
  a way that FORECLOSES inserting such a gate later — concretely, [CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY]'s
  kernel-served bus list is already the single seam a filter would attach to, so no redesign is implied
  by adding one.
- v1-scope: no login, no account model, no per-account namespace lock, unfiltered tenant list to any
  connecting peer that can reach the socket. Network-level trust (who can reach the listen port at all)
  is the ENTIRE v1 access-control surface, and it is explicitly OUTSIDE this spec (a deployment/ops
  concern, not a connector concern).
- rationale: David's explicit ruling — a login ceremony, multiple accounts, and per-account
  namespace-locking are overkill for the current stage, and building them now would spend effort on a
  surface nothing yet depends on. Reserving the SEAM (the kernel-served bus list,
  [CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY]) rather than designing the gate costs this spec nothing and forecloses
  nothing later.
- alternatives: (a) design and build a full login/account gate in v1 — rejected, verbatim overkill for
  now; (b) design the gate's SHAPE without building it — not chosen either, because the gate's shape
  depends on decisions (which identity provider, which account model) this session did not make and
  designing it prematurely risks constraining a later, better-informed choice; (c) reserve the position
  only, naming the seam it would attach to — CHOSEN, see `what-is-reserved` above.
- invalidating_assumption: "That the deployment is local/trusted. The moment this WS endpoint is
  exposed to an untrusted network, the v1 no-auth position inverts and [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE] must be
  resolved before that exposure, not after."
- status: open
- status-detail: deferred — reserved position, not designed; David's ruling fixes v1 scope, the GATE
  ITSELF is not designed here
- provenance: david-ruling — "Auth is OUT of v1 (David, verbatim: a login ceremony, multiple accounts,
  per-account namespace-locking are 'way way overkill for right now'). RESERVE the position as a
  named [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE] deferred fork (login = a server-level gate that filters the bus list); v1 is
  local/trusted, unfiltered list, no auth, no accounts, no permissions." (session ruling #6.)
- coupled-deferral: David's 2026-08-24 Q3 answer ([DEC-WS-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-24]) explicitly couples
  [CXC-WS-IDENTITY-POLICY]'s `localStorage <-> NVS` identity persistence to THIS gate — persistence is
  deferred until a login mechanism exists, not shipped ahead of it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [CXC-WS-ENTRY-OPERATOR], [CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY], [CXC-WS-IDENTITY-POLICY]

