## 10. Retained-state replay — generalized to the connector layer

### [CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY] MUST replay current state on subscribe; states the requirement at the CONNECTOR layer so UART/CAN inherit it, not just WS

- requirement: (MUST, testable) HARD requirement — when a WS peer's subscription (declared or widened
  by a switch) newly matches a namespace that already carries a value on the bound tenant bus (i.e.
  `bus.get_namespace(name).value is not None`, enumerable via the `bus.namespaces` property filtered
  through the SAME `pattern_matches()` helper `subscribe_pattern()` itself uses), the connector MUST
  synthesize and deliver ONE `BusMessage` per such namespace carrying its CURRENT value, marked
  `retained: True` — the SAME receive-side annotation MQTT's native retained delivery already sets on
  `BusMessage`, so a consumer widget cannot tell a WS-replayed value from an MQTT-retained one without
  inspecting `retained` (which is the point: identical consumer-side handling regardless of substrate).
- requirement: (MUST, testable) replay MUST occur exactly once per newly-matched namespace per
  subscribe/switch event — never on every publish thereafter (that is ordinary live delivery, not
  replay) and never re-sent for a namespace the peer was ALREADY subscribed to before this event.
- generalizes-to-the-connector-layer: (architect-synthesis, the load-bearing framing of this node) MQTT
  peers get current state for free via the broker's native retained-message mechanism; NO other
  substrate in this platform has an equivalent broker-side feature — UART and CAN have no retained
  concept at all. Without an explicit replay step, a non-broker peer's view renders BLANK until the
  next publish, which on an [DD-SUB-SUPERSET]-honoring event-driven namespace (quiet-bus-by-construction,
  per `Cadence.EVENT_DRIVEN`) may be minutes or hours. This requirement is therefore stated at the
  CONNECTOR layer, not the WS-transport layer specifically: a future UART or CAN connector inherits the
  SAME obligation from this node rather than each connector re-deriving it independently and some
  silently omitting it.
- negative-control-default-deny-still-holds: replay MUST NOT bypass [CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION]'s
  default-deny — a namespace the peer has NOT declared interest in is never replayed, regardless of
  whether it carries a current value. Replay widens WHEN a matched namespace is delivered (immediately,
  rather than waiting for the next publish); it never widens WHAT is delivered.
- negative-control-scope-still-honored: replay MUST honor `scope` on the replayed value exactly as a
  live publish would — a `scope: local` namespace on the peer's bound tenant is never replayed to a
  peer whose connection crosses a scope boundary the live publish path would also refuse, mirroring
  [CXC-SUB-SCOPE]'s publish-path enforcement rather than treating replay as a scope-exempt shortcut.
- verify-machine: a namespace is published on tenant T BEFORE a peer connects; the peer connects,
  binds T, and declares a matching pattern; asserts exactly ONE message arrives for that namespace with
  `retained == True` and the CURRENT value, with NO further publish having occurred; a second identical
  subscribe-widen for the same already-covered pattern produces NO duplicate replay.
- verify-human: David or composeio-first-bringup opens a real browser tab against a real tenant whose
  namespaces were last published minutes earlier (before the tab opened) and confirms the view shows
  CURRENT values immediately on load — never a blank/zero state that only populates on the next
  incidental publish.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — "Retained-state replay on subscribe, GENERALIZED to the connector
  layer: MQTT peers get current state free via native retained; non-broker transports (WS, UART, CAN)
  must replay it explicitly ... HARD requirement: without it a bus_view renders blank until the next
  publish. Negative-control the default-deny (an unsubscribed namespace NEVER reaches the peer) and the
  scope-honoring on publish (local tenant chatter does not leak)." (session architect-synthesis #9.)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION], [CXC-SUB-SCOPE], [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]

---

## 11. Peer identity — provisioning policy, localStorage as the browser's NVS

### [CXC-WS-IDENTITY-POLICY] DEFERRED for v1 — no identity persistence ships; the per-peer provisioning policy is described for when [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE] lands

- requirement: (MUST, testable) v1 MUST NOT persist any peer identity across reconnects — no
  `localStorage`/NVS-equivalent read or write on the connect path. Every WS peer, publish-capable or
  not, connects anonymously in v1; a connector implementation MAY assign a per-connection identity for
  logging/debug purposes only, and that identity MUST NOT be required to be stable across reconnects.
  This mirrors [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE]'s v1 scope (no login, no accounts, no permissions) — identity
  persistence is an authentication-adjacent concern and is deferred with it, not shipped ahead of it.
- deferred-policy-description: (architect-synthesis, describing the policy for WHEN a login mechanism
  exists — NOT a v1 requirement) once [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE] is resolved, the intended per-peer provisioning
  policy is: a WS peer that PUBLISHES (control shape, [CXC-WS-SINGLETON-PEER]'s pub+sub case — the peer
  must be addressable back, e.g. to correlate a request/response or to be named in a `src` field another
  peer reacts to) persists a stable identity across reconnects, using the browser's `localStorage` (or
  equivalent durable client-side store) in the SAME role an ESP32-class device's NVS plays for its own
  stable id ([CXC-ER-DEVICE]'s config-fetch/device-identity model) — generate-once, persist, reuse on
  every reconnect, never regenerate per session; a WS peer that is a PURE REALIZATION SURFACE
  (subscribe-only, read-only view, [CXC-CBK-PERIPHERAL-PLUGIN-MODEL]'s subscribe-side case) MAY remain
  anonymous even after the login mechanism lands, and the policy SHOULD be a property of the PEER's
  role, not a global connector setting. This description is kept so the future gate has a concrete
  starting shape; it binds nothing in v1.
- why-coupled-to-auth-gate: (architect-synthesis) David's 2026-08-24 Q3 answer
  ([DEC-WS-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-24]) deferred `localStorage <-> NVS` identity persistence until a
  login mechanism exists — David's own hedge on the idea (line 25961): "We *could* use localStorage <->
  NVS. But...if I connect to ComposeServer, it seems like the natural first thing to do... would be to
  present a list of ComposeBusses." Without a login/account model, a persisted client-side identity has
  nothing to authenticate against and no account to correlate to, so this node's obligation is
  EXPLICITLY coupled to [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE] rather than standing alone.
- negative-control: v1 never generates or reads a stored id on connect — a control-capable peer that
  publishes on its FIRST-EVER connection is attributed by its ephemeral per-connection identity only
  (if any), never a persisted one; two reconnects of the SAME browser tab MAY present two DIFFERENT
  per-connection identities in v1, and this is correct v1 behavior, not a defect.
- verify-machine: a v1 connector's connect path performs no `localStorage`/persistent-store read or
  write; a simulated peer that reconnects twice is never required to present the same identity value
  twice.
- verify-human: none required for v1 — the deferred policy has no v1 human-observable behavior to gate;
  [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE]'s own future acceptance table owns this once resolved.
- status: deferred
- status-detail: deferred — coupled to [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE]; v1 mandates NO identity persistence
- provenance: architect-synthesis (original mechanics, session architect-synthesis #10: "Peer identity =
  per-peer provisioning policy (localStorage <-> NVS): a peer that must be addressed back persists a
  stable id; a pure realization surface (read-only view) may be anonymous — the same policy device
  identity already uses.") + david-ruling (2026-08-24 Q3 answer downgrading this from a v1 MUST to
  deferred, per [DEC-WS-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-24]). The specific citation to [CXC-ER-DEVICE] as the
  existing device-identity policy this generalizes is this spec-writer's own addition, made while
  grounding the dispatch's analogy in a real corpus node rather than an unnamed one.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [CXC-ER-DEVICE], [CXC-CBK-PERIPHERAL-PLUGIN-MODEL], [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE]

---

## 12. The connector interface — what composeio-expert builds

### [CXC-WS-CONNECTOR-INTERFACE] The concrete module, factory signature, and peer object shape

- module: a NEW module, `composeio/transport_ws.py` — sibling to `transport_mqtt.py`/`transport_can.py`/
  `transport_uart.py`, registered into `transport_registry.py`'s existing `_FACTORIES` exactly as they
  are (`register("ws", _ws)`), imported LAZILY per the registry's own stated discipline (one missing
  optional dependency — a WS server library — must never break the other four registered factories).
- factory-signature: `_ws(spec: dict, app_id: str) -> WSConnector`, mirroring `_mqtt`/`_can`/`_uart`'s
  existing shape exactly (`spec` carries `host`, `port`, and any TLS/listen-backlog config; `app_id` is
  accepted for signature consistency with the other four factories even though a LISTEN-role connector
  has less use for it than a DIAL-role one — noted so a reviewer does not read its presence as
  unexplained).
- ws-connector-class: `WSConnector` — NOT a `Transport` ABC subclass in the DIAL sense
  `MQTTTransport`/`CANTransport`/`UARTTransport` are; it owns an accept loop and a collection of peer
  objects, one per accepted connection ([CXC-WS-SINGLETON-PEER]). Its public surface: `connectors()`
  (or equivalent enumeration a kernel `attach_connector()` call can register, per
  [CXC-CBK-KERNEL-PRIMITIVES]'s existing primitive), and a `peers` property enumerating live peer
  objects for status/debug surfaces (`_server/status`, mirroring [CXC-CSL-STATUS-SURFACE]'s
  already-built pattern for the plugin loader).
- ws-peer-class: `WSPeer` (or equivalent) — one instance per accepted connection, owning: `state`
  (`PRE-BINDING` | `BOUND`, [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]); `bound_tenant: str | None`; `declared_patterns: list[str]`
  (fed to `subscribe_pattern()` against the bound tenant's bus, [CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION]); `identity:
  str | None` ([CXC-WS-IDENTITY-POLICY]). It exposes `bind(tenant_id)`, `switch(tenant_id)`, and
  `close()`, each implementing [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]'s ordered steps.
- reuse-by-reference: (what this module does NOT reimplement) `subscribe_pattern()`'s pattern matching
  and enforcement-report machinery ([CXC-SUB-DECLARE]/[CXC-SUB-ENFORCE], `bus.py::is_pattern`/
  `pattern_matches`/`validate_pattern`); `BusMessage.to_json()`/`from_json()` (`transport.py`, verbatim,
  [CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT]); per-tenant bus resolution (`ComposeServer`'s existing `tenant_bus()`/
  multi-tenant orchestration, [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY]); the `bus.namespaces` property and each
  `Namespace.value` accessor for retained-replay enumeration ([CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY]). This module's
  entire net-new surface is: the accept loop, per-connection peer state and lifecycle transitions, the
  deep-link designator parse ([DD-WS-DEEPLINK-URL], open), and the replay-trigger wiring around an
  otherwise-unmodified `subscribe_pattern()` call.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT], [CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION], [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE], [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY]

---

## 13. Explicit out-of-scope — cited, not specified

### [CXC-WS-OUT-OF-SCOPE] The three units this spec deliberately does not design

- the-operator-console: the cross-tenant OPERATOR CONSOLE — the bus-registry's CONTENT (what metadata
  each tenant entry carries), its selection UX, and the reserved auth gate's concrete mechanism — is
  its own kernel-scoped unit. This spec's obligation to it is narrow and explicit: serve SOME bus list
  in `PRE-BINDING` ([CXC-WS-ENTRY-OPERATOR]) and leave the seam a future auth gate would attach to
  unforeclosed ([DD-WS-AUTH-GATE]).
- the-render-backend: the ComposeUX HTML/DOM render backend (render-engineer's domain,
  [DEC-COMPOSEIO-EXTENSIONS-ROADMAP-2026-08] item 9's `bus_views`) consumes THIS connector's wire
  format ([CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT]) but its own rendering, widget-binding, and control-publish mechanics
  are entirely out of scope here.
- scene-bundle-serving: how a `bus_views` scene bundle itself is fetched/served (the config-over-bus /
  `bus_storage` seam, [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS]) is a separate, already-partially-specified concern; this
  connector carries live namespace traffic once a scene is already running, not the scene's own
  delivery.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis, restating the dispatch's explicit scope split (ratified (ii) this
  session) as a queryable node rather than leaving it only in frontmatter prose.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [DEC-COMPOSEIO-EXTENSIONS-ROADMAP-2026-08], [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS]

---

## 14. Error and boundary matrix

### [CXC-WS-BOUNDARIES] Every condition a builder would otherwise have to invent an answer for

| # | Condition | Required behaviour |
|---|-----------|--------------------|
| 1 | Unknown `type:` (not `ws`) reaches this connector's registration path | N/A to this connector — `transport_registry.build()` already raises `UnknownTransportError` naming `known_types()` ([CXC-WS-CONNECTOR-NOT-PLUGIN]) |
| 2 | `type: ws` declared but the WS server backend cannot bind (port in use, no permission) | CORRECTED 2026-08-25 (diamond-remediation, first-bringup finding): does NOT surface from the factory. `_ws()` only CONSTRUCTS the `WSConnector` object — no socket work happens there. The bind happens LATER, on a background thread, inside `start()`. `start()` MUST raise IMMEDIATELY, carrying the underlying `OSError`'s own text, the instant the accept-socket bind fails — never a 5s timeout, never a silent `None` return from the factory. WS is the ONE LISTEN-role connector in the registry; every other (DIAL-role) factory's "known type, unreachable backend -> return `None`" contract (`transport_registry.py`'s rule 2, [CXC-WS-BOUNDARIES] row 1's sibling rule) does not transfer to it unchanged, because a DIAL-role factory attempts its connection synchronously inside the factory call itself and this one does not — see [CXC-WS-CONNECTOR-INTERFACE] |
| 3 | A message arrives while `PRE-BINDING` | ignored for tenant traffic purposes; only the bus-selection exchange is legal in this state ([CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]) |
| 4 | Empty declared subscription set | subscribe NOTHING; `*` must be explicit ([CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION], inherited from [CXC-SUB-DECLARE]) |
| 5 | A declared pattern matches a namespace on a DIFFERENT tenant than the peer is bound to | never delivered — bound-tenant resolution happens before pattern matching, never across tenants ([CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY]) |
| 6 | Deep-link designator names a nonexistent tenant | fail loudly, named error; never silent fallback to the operator list or a default tenant ([CXC-WS-ENTRY-DEEPLINK]) |
| 7 | A switch is requested while already `PRE-BINDING` (no bound tenant to leave) | treated as a plain `bind`, not a `switch` — there is no A to leave ([CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]) |
| 8 | A value fails `BusMessage`'s JSON-serialisability check (raw bytes) | reject identically to every other JSON substrate — `UnserialisableValue`, never a base64 escape hatch ([CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT]) |
| 9 | A namespace matched by a widened subscription already carries a value | replay it once, marked `retained: True`, before any further live traffic for that namespace ([CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY]) |
| 10 | The SAME namespace is matched by an already-existing subscription AND a newly-declared one (overlapping patterns) | replay exactly once, never duplicated per overlapping pattern ([CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY]) |
| 11 | A `scope: local` namespace on the bound tenant would be replayed to a peer the live-publish path would refuse | refuse the replay identically to the live-publish refusal — replay is not a scope-exempt shortcut ([CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY]) |
| 12 | A control-capable peer connects (v1 — identity persistence deferred) | v1 attributes the publish to an ephemeral per-connection identity only; no persisted id is generated or required ([CXC-WS-IDENTITY-POLICY], deferred to [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE]) |
| 13 | Two tabs from the same browser (v1 — no persisted identity) | remain TWO independent peer objects regardless ([CXC-WS-SINGLETON-PEER]); v1 assigns no shared persisted identity between them — that correlation is deferred with [CXC-WS-IDENTITY-POLICY] to [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE] |
| 14 | An untrusted network can reach the listen port | OUTSIDE this spec's v1 access-control surface entirely; [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE]'s invalidating_assumption fires and the deferred gate must be resolved before that exposure ships |

- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]

---

## 15. Acceptance — machine signal + glass gate

### [CXC-WS-ACCEPTANCE] The connector's two-tier verdict — a machine signal AND, for every failure class human eyes can catch that a narrow test cannot, an explicit human/glass gate

- definition: per [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE] and the loader-spec precedent ([CXC-CSL-ACCEPTANCE]),
  machine-green alone never accepts this connector — the failure classes that matter most here (a
  cross-tenant leak, a blank-until-next-publish view, a stale-looking switch) are exactly the ones a
  narrow unit test can pass while the real browser experience is broken. [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS]
  applies throughout this table.

| ID | Requirement | verify-machine | verify-human (glass) |
|----|-------------|-----------------|------------------------|
| WS-V1 | Registered as `type: ws` connector, never a plugin ([CXC-WS-CONNECTOR-NOT-PLUGIN]) | `known_types()` includes `ws`; `build({"type":"ws",...})` returns a connector, never silently `None` on a valid spec | none required |
| WS-V2 | Each connection is one singleton persistent peer ([CXC-WS-SINGLETON-PEER]) | two simultaneous connections yield two independent peer identities/state | two real browser tabs behave independently; closing one leaves the other untouched |
| WS-V3 | Lifecycle states are exactly PRE-BINDING/BOUND, ordered correctly ([CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]) | no tenant traffic before bind; subscribe-then-replay ordering on bind; explicit leave-A before join-B on switch | a real switch shows the new tenant's current state with no old-tenant residue |
| WS-V4 | One tenant at a time, kernel-served cross-tenant list ([CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY]) | same-named namespace on two tenants never cross-delivers to a bound peer | operator confirms no overlap frame during a real switch |
| WS-V5 | Operator entry mode serves the list before any data ([CXC-WS-ENTRY-OPERATOR]) | non-empty list served pre-selection; peer stays PRE-BINDING until selection | a fresh tab shows a picker before live data |
| WS-V6 | Deep-link entry mode converges on the same BOUND state ([CXC-WS-ENTRY-DEEPLINK]) | valid designator reaches BOUND with no list ever sent; invalid designator fails loudly, never falls through | a real deep-link URL lands directly on the named tenant |
| WS-V7 | v1 ships with no auth, position reserved not foreclosed ([DD-WS-AUTH-GATE]) | no login/account code path exists; the bus-list seam [CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY] serves is the one a gate would attach to | n/a — structural / architectural review |
| WS-V8 | Wire envelope is `BusMessage.to_json()`/`from_json()` verbatim ([CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT]) | round-trip equality on every field combination; raw-bytes value rejected identically to other JSON substrates | none required |
| WS-V9 | Subscription is explicit, default-deny, no tenant firehose ([CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION]) | a WS-specific negative-control test now (`on_message()`+`pattern_matches()` filter); [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]'s SUB-C1 suite does NOT exist yet — CORRECTED 2026-08-25, [DEFECT-SPEC-88-CXC-WS-EVIDENCE-1C623F] — once authored, WS becomes one substrate row in it | none required — covered by SUB-C1's own human gate once that suite exists |
| WS-V10 | Retained state replays once on subscribe/switch, never leaks past default-deny/scope ([CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY]) | pre-existing value replays exactly once marked `retained: True`; undeclared/local-scoped namespaces never replay | a real tab shows current state immediately on load, never blank-until-next-publish |
| WS-V11 | Peer identity — v1 ships with NO persistence, deferred to [DD-WS-AUTH-GATE] ([CXC-WS-IDENTITY-POLICY]) | connect path performs no `localStorage`/persistent-store read or write | n/a for v1 — structural/architectural review only |
| WS-V12 | Placement recorded, not silent ([CXC-WS-PLACEMENT]) | the spec carries the `[PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED]` node + the filename-check property | n/a — structural |

- requirement: WS-V4 (the cross-tenant boundary) is the gate for the FIRM part of
  [CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY] — a bound peer leaking another tenant's traffic AT ANY INSTANT is UNSAFE and
  MUST NOT ship, because [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY] is LOCKED PERMANENTLY and no build may cross it,
  mirroring [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]'s SUB-C1 gate discipline exactly. This gate does NOT extend to
  foreclosing [CXC-WS-TENANT-BOUNDARY]'s own `invalidating_assumption`: David flagged the LARGER
  one-tenant-per-connection MODEL as re-litigable once real multi-tenancy deployments exist (line
  25921) — WS-V4 asserts only that a peer never sees a second tenant's traffic while this model is in
  force, never that the model itself is permanently unrevisable.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE], [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS], [CXC-CSL-ACCEPTANCE]

