## 17. Open forks — flagged for the architect's / David's ratify

### [DD-WS-DEEPLINK-URL] OPEN — the deep-link URL shape is unspecified

- question: [CXC-WS-ENTRY-DEEPLINK] specifies that a deep-link designator MUST exist and MUST converge
  on the same `BOUND` sequence as the operator path — it does not specify the URL SHAPE itself (a path
  segment, `/bus/<tenant_id>`? a query parameter, `?tenant=<id>`? a subdomain?).
- why-left-open: `WSPeer.bind(tenant_id)` ([CXC-WS-CONNECTOR-INTERFACE]) takes an already-RESOLVED
  `tenant_id` string — the function signature does not require the URL shape to be decided to be
  implementable and testable; only the (unspecified) code path that PARSES a URL into that string
  needs it.
- candidates: (not a recommendation, offered so the fork is concrete) (a) a path segment,
  `wss://host/bus/<tenant_id>`, cheapest to route at a reverse proxy; (b) a query parameter,
  `wss://host/ws?tenant=<tenant_id>`, avoids proxy path-rewriting rules; (c) a subdomain,
  `<tenant_id>.host`, gives each tenant a stable externally-visible identity at the cost of DNS/TLS
  provisioning per tenant.
- decision: OPEN — not yet decided.
- rationale: see `why-left-open` above.
- alternatives: see `candidates` above.
- invalidating_assumption: once the operator console ([CXC-WS-OUT-OF-SCOPE]) needs to GENERATE
  shareable deep-link URLs for an operator to hand out, the choice stops being deferrable — the
  console's own UX depends on which shape was picked.
- build-implemented-default: (diamond-remediation, 2026-08-25) the build implemented candidate (a) —
  `/bus/<tenant_id>` — as a SWAPPABLE DEFAULT, not a ratify. It is wired through an INJECTED
  `deeplink_parser` (dependency-injected, not hardcoded into the connector), so a future ratify of (b)
  or (c) is a parser swap, not a connector rewrite — the fork stays open at the SPEC level even though
  the build needed to pick something to ship against. This does not close the fork; it records what the
  build is running today pending David's actual ratify.
- status: open
- status-detail: open — flagged for architect/David ratify; the build runs candidate (a) as a swappable
  default meanwhile (see `build-implemented-default`)
- provenance: architect-proposed — the fork is named explicitly in the dispatch that produced this
  spec ("[DD-WS-DEEPLINK-URL] (the deep-link URL shape)"); the candidate list is this spec-writer's own
  addition, offered to make the fork concrete, not a recommendation. The `build-implemented-default`
  property is diamond-remediation, recording what the build actually shipped, not a new ratify.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [CXC-WS-ENTRY-DEEPLINK], [CXC-WS-CONNECTOR-INTERFACE]

### [DD-WS-SWITCH-UX] OPEN — the focal-bus switch mechanism, referenced to the operator-console unit

- question: [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE] specifies the SWITCH's required ORDERING (leave-A, intermediate state,
  join-B) but not how an operator TRIGGERS a switch in the UI, nor whether the declared subscription
  set is silently carried across (this spec's default) or offered as a re-declare step.
- why-left-open: this is squarely the cross-tenant OPERATOR CONSOLE's own unit
  ([CXC-WS-OUT-OF-SCOPE]) — its selection UX is out of scope here by the session's own ratified scope
  split; this spec specifies only the CONNECTOR-side mechanics the console's UI would call into.
- decision: OPEN — deferred to the operator-console unit, not decided here.
- rationale: see `why-left-open` above.
- alternatives: not enumerated here — belongs to the console unit's own design pass.
- invalidating_assumption: if the connector-side default (carry the declared set unchanged across a
  switch, [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]) proves wrong once real console UX is designed — e.g. if switching tenants
  should ALWAYS prompt a fresh subscription choice — [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]'s switch requirement would need
  a revisit, not just the console.
- status: open
- status-detail: open — flagged for architect/David ratify, deferred to the operator-console unit
- provenance: architect-proposed — the fork is named explicitly in the dispatch ("[DD-WS-SWITCH-UX]
  (the focal-bus switch mechanism — referenced to the operator-console unit)").
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE], [CXC-WS-OUT-OF-SCOPE]

### [DD-WS-CONTROL-FRAMES] DECIDED — the `ws`-discriminated control vocabulary shipped and landed on `origin/main`

- question: (diamond-remediation, 2026-08-25) [CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT] freezes the `BusMessage` bus-DATA
  envelope (`ns`, `type`, `src`, `ts`, `scope`, `seq`, `val`/`evt`/`data`) verbatim — it said nothing
  about the CONTROL channel a WS peer also needs, distinct from bus-data traffic: the bus-selection
  exchange ([CXC-WS-ENTRY-OPERATOR]/[CXC-WS-ENTRY-DEEPLINK]), the subscribe declaration itself
  ([CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION]), the switch request ([CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]'s SWITCH), and — once
  [SPEC:CXC-DOM] rides this connector — the form-validation request/response
  ([CXC-DOM-SERVER-VALIDATION]). Neither this spec nor any other named a concrete, discriminated frame
  shape for any of these.
- why-left-open: this spec's silence on the control vocabulary was not a deliberate deferral — it was
  an unnoticed gap. Both the WS build and the [SPEC:CXC-DOM] build each invented their OWN control-frame
  vocabulary independently, because neither had a shared one to converge on. That is exactly the
  silent-drift shape [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS] warns against for bus-DATA traffic; this spec simply never
  named the control-frame boundary as needing the same discipline, so nobody built to a shared contract.
  Now resolved — see `decision` below.
- correction-to-the-original-what-exists: the pre-integration `what-exists` slot on this node described
  the control channel as `op`-discriminated (a `{op: ..., ...}` shape). That was superseded by the
  landed integration: the SETTLED discriminator key is **`ws`**, not `op` — DOM did not invent a second
  discriminator; it reused `transport_ws.py`'s own `ws` key end-to-end, layering `req_id`
  request-correlation on top for its own multi-outstanding-request needs. This correction is itself the
  point of resolving this node rather than leaving the stale `op` description in the corpus.
- decision: DECIDED — the settled `ws`-discriminated control-frame vocabulary, verified against
  `python/composeio/transport_ws.py` (`WSPeer.handle_frame`/`handle_bus_frame`, landed `origin/main`
  commit `d95aee1` and its ancestors) and `python/composeio/render/dom/server.py` +
  `python/composeio/render/dom/static/ws_client.js` (the DOM-layer extension riding the same channel):
  - **discriminator**: the frame carries either a `ws` key (a control frame) or an `ns` key (a bus-data
    `BusMessage` frame, per [CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT]) — never both, never a third shape
    (`WSPeer.handle_frame`: `verb = wire.get("ws")`; `verb is None` routes to `handle_bus_frame`).
  - **client -> server verbs** (all landed in `WSPeer.handle_frame`): `list` (request the operator
    tenant list, [CXC-WS-ENTRY-OPERATOR]), `select` (bind to a tenant, [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]), `switch`
    (leave-A/pre-binding/join-B, [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE]'s SWITCH), `subscribe` (declare the subscription
    set — **full-set-replace**, not incremental: `set_subscription()` replaces `self.declared_patterns`
    wholesale each call, then replays only newly-matched namespaces against the PRIOR set,
    [CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY]).
  - **server -> client verbs**: `tenants` (the operator list, `{ws:"tenants", tenants:[...]}`), `bound`
    (`{ws:"bound", tenant:...}`), `prebinding` (the observable leave-A intermediate state during a
    switch, `{ws:"prebinding"}`), `subscribed` (the enforcement report, `{ws:"subscribed",
    patterns:[...], enforcement:"broker"}`, [CXC-SUB-REPORT]), `error` (`{ws:"error", code:...,
    message:...}`) with codes `unknown_tenant` (an unknown/unselectable tenant, `UnknownTenantError`),
    `not_bound` (`handle_bus_frame` called while `PRE-BINDING`), `bad_envelope` (a bus-data frame that
    fails `BusMessage.from_json()`), `unknown_verb` (an unrecognized `ws` value — this is also the code a
    DOM-only verb like `validate_config` receives from the production listener, see below),
    `bad_request` (a `ValueError` from a control-verb handler, e.g. a malformed subscribe pattern),
    `bad_frame` (the raw frame was not a JSON object at all).
  - **bus frames**: faithful `BusMessage` `{ns, type, src, ts, scope, seq, val?/evt?/data?}`
    ([CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT], unchanged by this node). ⛔ **`type` in `{state, event}` is FROZEN
    vocabulary** — `composeio/transport.py`'s `MessageType` Enum has exactly two members
    (`STATE = "state"`, `EVENT = "event"`), and `BusMessage.from_json()` raises
    `UnknownMessageTypeName` on any other value; it is never silently coerced to `state`. Recording WHY
    this matters here: a DOM/browser build that ever publishes a message with an unallocated `type`
    (e.g. a `type: "state_change"` shaped after a DOM `state_changed`-style event name rather than the
    frozen enum) is REFUSED at `handle_bus_frame` (`bad_envelope`) rather than silently downgraded —
    every browser-to-bus publish using such a value is silently refused end-to-end unless the caller
    checks for the `bad_envelope` error frame, which is exactly why this frozen-vocabulary boundary is
    named explicitly here rather than left implicit. Retained values add `retained: true` to the
    envelope (`BusMessage.retained: bool = False`, [CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY]).
  - **DOM-layer extension, NOT a transport verb**: `validate_config` -> `config_result` (server-side
    ComposeServer config validation, [SPEC:CXC-DOM]) rides the SAME `{ws:..., req_id:...}` channel —
    `python/composeio/render/dom/server.py` and `.../static/ws_client.js` both say so explicitly
    ("`validate_config -> config_result` is a DOM-LAYER extension, NOT a `transport_ws.py` verb — the
    production WS listener does not serve it, `unknown_verb` for it"). This node's own connector
    ([SPEC:CXC-WS], `transport_ws.py`) legitimately rejects `validate_config` with `unknown_verb` — that
    is CORRECT behavior, not a bug the two specs need to reconcile; [SPEC:CXC-DOM]'s own server owns
    that verb, this spec's connector does not and should not.
- rationale: verified directly against the landed `transport_ws.py` frame-handling code and the DOM
  render backend's own seam-note comments (both cited above), not re-derived from memory or from either
  diamond leg's prior report — per this spec's own [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE] discipline, "the code
  is the truth."
- alternatives: not enumerated — this records what was actually built and landed, not a design choice
  among unbuilt candidates.
- invalidating_assumption: if a future change to `transport_ws.py` or `render/dom/server.py` adds,
  renames, or removes a `ws`-verb or an error code, this node's vocabulary table goes stale the same way
  the pre-integration `op`-description did — the next spec-writer pass touching either file MUST re-verify
  this node against the code rather than trust it silently.
- status: decided
- status-detail: decided — the vocabulary is landed and verified against `origin/main`
  (`python/composeio/transport_ws.py`, `python/composeio/render/dom/server.py`,
  `python/composeio/render/dom/static/ws_client.js`); the WS<->DOM integration pass this node was waiting
  on has reported.
- provenance: architect-synthesis — diamond-remediation finding recorded this gap; this resolution is a
  factlattice-editor corpus-hygiene pass verifying the actual landed code against the vocabulary summary
  supplied for this edit and correcting the one place they disagreed (`op` -> `ws` discriminator).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-WS]
- see-also: [CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT], [CXC-WS-ENTRY-OPERATOR], [CXC-WS-LIFECYCLE], [CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION],
  [CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY], [SPEC:CXC-DOM]

